    createStaticVNode('<h1 id="_6-scratch-example" tabindex="-1">6. Scratch Example <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-scratch-example" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. Scratch Example&quot;">​</a></h1><p>[TOC]</p><h2 id="_6-1-preparation" tabindex="-1">6.1 Preparation <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-1-preparation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6.1 Preparation&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This example uses the CoreX controller and the 4-ch line follower sensor. Connect the sensor to Port 4 on the CoreX controller with a 4-pin cable.</p><img loading="lazy" height="430" width="549" src="' + _imports_0 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:center;">Pin</th><th style="text-align:center;">Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:center;">5V</td><td style="text-align:center;">Power input</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;">GND</td><td style="text-align:center;">Power ground</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;">SDA</td><td style="text-align:center;">Serial data transmission between devices</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;">SCL</td><td style="text-align:center;">Clock pulse generation</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="note custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">NOTE</p><p></p><p><strong>The detection distance can be adjusted with the knobs, allowing the sensitivity to be tuned for different environments. For calibration, first place the infrared probes over a black area and adjust the knobs until all blue indicator lights turn on. Then slowly rotate the knobs until the lights just turn off. Next, place the probes over a white area and continue adjusting until the lights just turn on. The probes are now set to the optimal sensitivity.</strong></p></div><img loading="lazy" height="331" width="659" src="' + _imports_1 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><h2 id="_6-2-example-program" tabindex="-1">6.2 Example Program <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-2-example-program" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6.2 Example Program&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The 4-ch line follower sensor works by infrared detection. The sensor includes four probes, and each probe contains one receiver and one transmitter.</p><ol><li><p>Click <strong>WonderCode</strong> to open the programming software.</p></li><li><p>Click <strong>File</strong> at the top of the software, then select <strong>Load from Computer</strong> to open the directory and import the program. The example program can also be dragged directly into the software window.</p><img loading="lazy" height="893" width="1386" src="' + _imports_2 + '" class="common_img" style="width:700px;"></li><li><p>Connect the CoreX controller to the computer with a USB cable. Then check the COM port under <strong>Connect</strong> and click it to connect. <strong>COM5</strong> is used here as an example. Select the actual port shown on the computer.</p></li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="893" width="1386" src="' + _imports_3 + '" class="common_img" style="width:700px;"><ol start="4"><li><p>After the connection is complete, click the download button in the upper-right corner to download the program to the CoreX controller.</p><img loading="lazy" height="350" width="757" src="' + _imports_4 + '" class="common_img" style="width:700px;"></li></ol><h2 id="_6-3-program-outcome" tabindex="-1">6.3 Program Outcome <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-3-program-outcome" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6.3 Program Outcome&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><p>Prepare a sheet of white paper with a black line, or draw several black lines on white paper.</p></li><li><p>Aim the sensor probes at the black and white lines. When one of the sensors detects a black line, the CoreX controller lights up the corresponding color on its onboard LED.</p></li></ol><p>For example, when Sensor 1 detects a black line, the LED turns red. When Sensor 2 detects a black line, the LED turns green. When Sensor 3 detects a black line, the LED turns blue. When Sensor 4 detects a black line, the LED turns white.</p>', 16)
